{"name":"Bonsai Trunk","hex":"#6c6d62","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bonsai","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A tree or plant that has been miniaturized by planting it in a small pot, restricting its roots, and by careful pruning.","The art of planting and growing trees or plants in such a manner."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make into a bonsai by restricting the roots and pruning."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bonsai"},{"term":"trunk","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Part of a body.\n The usually single, more or less upright part of a tree, between the roots and the branches.","The usually single, more or less upright part of a tree, between the roots and the branches."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To lop off; to curtail; to truncate.","To extract (ores) from the slimes in which they are contained, by means of a trunk."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/trunk"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bonsai","blocks":[{"text":"Bonsai is the Japanese art of growing and shaping miniature trees in containers, with a long documented history of influences and native Japanese development over a thousand years, and with unique aesthetics, cultural history, and terminology derived from its evolution in Japan. Similar arts exist in other cultures, including Korea's bunjae, the Chinese art of penjing, and the miniature living landscapes of Vietnamese Hòn non bộ."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Bonsai%2C_Kenshin_Toge.jpg/330px-Bonsai%2C_Kenshin_Toge.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bonsai","wikidata":"Q64365"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Bonsai_tree2.jpg/500px-Bonsai_tree2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Bonsai_tree2.jpg/1280px-Bo